WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:11.356 --> 00:00:14.321
Okay, so before we start,
I wanted to introduce our speaker today.

00:00:15.730 --> 00:00:20.100
Kirsty Nathoo is the CFO and
a partner at Y Combinator.

00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:24.358
And she's basically worked virtually with
every company that has gone through YC.

00:00:24.358 --> 00:00:28.380
So from incorporation to
fundraising to hiring to exits,

00:00:28.380 --> 00:00:29.890
Kirsty's pretty much seen it all.

00:00:29.890 --> 00:00:32.973
And today she'll talk to you
about startup mechanics.

00:00:32.973 --> 00:00:34.465
&gt;&gt; All right, thank you.

00:00:34.465 --> 00:00:35.220
Hi, everyone.

00:00:35.220 --> 00:00:37.890
Thanks for coming in to listen to this.

00:00:37.890 --> 00:00:41.210
So as Steven said,
this lecture is about startup mechanics,

00:00:41.210 --> 00:00:46.010
which is going to cover some of the the
basic issues that most startups will face

00:00:46.010 --> 00:00:51.470
as they go through the early
days of becoming a real company.

00:00:51.470 --> 00:00:53.740
And I'm really only talking
about the basics here.

00:00:53.740 --> 00:00:55.590
There's a lot of complexity.

00:00:55.590 --> 00:00:57.850
And there's a lot of
other things that happen.

00:00:57.850 --> 00:01:02.950
But this will kind of give you some ideas
of the things you should know about.

00:01:02.950 --> 00:01:07.638
And some of the resources out there
that there is to help you with those.

00:01:07.638 --> 00:01:12.620
I know that we're standing here in
California at the moment, but a lot of

00:01:12.620 --> 00:01:17.948
this stuff will apply to any startups,
no matter where they are in the world.

00:01:17.948 --> 00:01:21.349
And I'll talk a little bit throughout
the session about things that

00:01:21.349 --> 00:01:24.940
are US-specific and things that
are specific to the rest of the world.

00:01:26.760 --> 00:01:31.330
And this is definitely not the glamorous
part of being a startup founder.

00:01:32.710 --> 00:01:35.940
You'll hear from all sorts of
people throughout the rest of this

00:01:35.940 --> 00:01:38.700
course about all the glamorous things and
all the other things.

00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:43.515
But these are the kind of nuts and bolts
that, if you take care of in the early

00:01:43.515 --> 00:01:48.637
days, you stick with the simple,
standard, basic processes, you don't try

00:01:48.637 --> 00:01:53.628
to reinvent new voting structures or
anything like that, it will be enough.

00:01:53.628 --> 00:01:57.740
On the flip side,
if you don't do some of these basics,

00:01:57.740 --> 00:02:02.728
then the company down the road will
come into some problems that will

00:02:02.728 --> 00:02:06.772
cause a lot of time, effort,
and money to get solved.

00:02:06.772 --> 00:02:09.695
And I'll bring up a few
stories as we go through,

00:02:09.695 --> 00:02:12.130
of situations where that's happened.

00:02:12.130 --> 00:02:18.180
So these are the topics that
we're going to cover today.

00:02:18.180 --> 00:02:21.780
So we're going to kind of go through
the life cycle of a company.

00:02:21.780 --> 00:02:24.290
So we're going to start in
the early days of forming,

00:02:24.290 --> 00:02:28.270
going through raising some money,
hiring some people, giving out shares.

00:02:31.260 --> 00:02:35.620
Okay, so the first step in this
is the formation of the company.

00:02:35.620 --> 00:02:40.620
And so
formation creates a separate legal entity.

00:02:40.620 --> 00:02:45.100
And what that means is an entity
that will pay its own taxes,

00:02:45.100 --> 00:02:51.050
be responsible for its own assets, for its
own liabilities, sign its own contracts.

00:02:51.050 --> 00:02:53.320
Sue and be sued.

00:02:53.320 --> 00:02:55.650
And it's all independent of the owners.

00:02:55.650 --> 00:02:58.890
So you, as a founder,
would be independent of the company.

00:03:00.220 --> 00:03:05.570
And in the US, this kind of company
is called a C-corp, a corporation.

00:03:05.570 --> 00:03:10.430
There are other structures that's
out there for entities to form.

00:03:10.430 --> 00:03:14.900
But in the case of startups
who are expecting to

00:03:14.900 --> 00:03:20.040
be successful and to grow and
to raise money, and so,

00:03:20.040 --> 00:03:24.740
to eventually IPO,
then a C-corp is the way to go.

00:03:24.740 --> 00:03:28.140
Investors can only really
invest in C-corps.

00:03:28.140 --> 00:03:32.640
There's a lot of other things that come in
if you decide to go down some other route.

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:36.660
And you may hear some advice from people
who aren't necessarily in the startup

00:03:36.660 --> 00:03:41.750
world to start with a LLC or
something like that,

00:03:41.750 --> 00:03:43.270
which might work in the short term.

00:03:43.270 --> 00:03:47.670
But then as you become more successful,

00:03:47.670 --> 00:03:51.070
more in need of raising money,
you will have to convert to a C-corp.

00:03:51.070 --> 00:03:54.666
So it probably makes sense if you
are thinking about doing a startup in that

00:03:54.666 --> 00:03:56.530
situation, to start with a C-corp.

00:03:58.120 --> 00:04:00.330
And terminology get's
a little bit loose here.

00:04:00.330 --> 00:04:02.550
And I'm as guilty of this as everyone.

00:04:02.550 --> 00:04:05.200
But through the whole of this session, and

00:04:05.200 --> 00:04:10.200
when we're generally talking about this,
basically the words company, C-corp,

00:04:10.200 --> 00:04:14.340
corporation, entity,
startup all mean the same thing.

00:04:14.340 --> 00:04:21.223
They all mean this separate
legal entity that is a C-corp.

00:04:21.223 --> 00:04:24.931
All right, the hardest point in formation
though is deciding actually when to

00:04:24.931 --> 00:04:26.291
incorporate this company.

00:04:26.291 --> 00:04:30.632
Because obviously, with a separate
legal entity comes a lot of admin and

00:04:30.632 --> 00:04:34.130
various other processes that
have to happen around that.

00:04:34.130 --> 00:04:39.040
And it's one of those situations where
you don't want to do it too soon.

00:04:39.040 --> 00:04:40.586
But you also don't want to do it too late.

00:04:40.586 --> 00:04:45.290
So too soon would probably be if
you're still throwing around ideas,

00:04:45.290 --> 00:04:48.890
you're still thinking about
this as a side project.

00:04:48.890 --> 00:04:52.450
You're not sure if you're going
to be doing this for a long time,

00:04:52.450 --> 00:04:56.229
you don't know if you're going to
be working with your cofounders.

00:04:56.229 --> 00:05:00.154
So all of those, I would suggest,
that you don't Incorporate at this point.

00:05:00.154 --> 00:05:05.134
However, the flip side of that is, if
you're at the stage where you are creating

00:05:05.134 --> 00:05:09.455
significant amounts of IP that you
want to make sure is clearly owned,

00:05:09.455 --> 00:05:13.339
particularly if there's more
than one of you creating the IP,

00:05:13.339 --> 00:05:17.544
Then that would be a situation where
you would want to incorporate.

00:05:17.544 --> 00:05:20.454
Another situation is if
you've built a product that

00:05:20.454 --> 00:05:23.825
you're now ready to start charging for
so that you can create

00:05:23.825 --> 00:05:28.408
a separate entity that takes the money in
for the products that you're charging.

00:05:28.408 --> 00:05:32.830
You want a separate bank account, you want
a company account and a personal account.

00:05:32.830 --> 00:05:36.320
You don't want to
intermingle those dollars.

00:05:36.320 --> 00:05:41.920
And actually,
Stripe has a product called Stripe Atlas,

00:05:41.920 --> 00:05:46.970
which basically helps founders to
form a company to the point where

00:05:46.970 --> 00:05:50.720
they can get a bank account and they can
start accepting payments through Stripe.

00:05:53.060 --> 00:05:57.741
The other reason why you would want to
incorporate is just to protect you as

00:05:57.741 --> 00:05:58.730
individuals.

00:05:58.730 --> 00:06:04.380
Because again, as I was saying, a separate
entity means that all of the liabilities,

00:06:04.380 --> 00:06:05.790
and anything else that happens there,

00:06:05.790 --> 00:06:08.040
would be on the company rather
than you as an individual.

00:06:08.040 --> 00:06:12.610
So if you get into the situation where
that might come important, again,

00:06:12.610 --> 00:06:14.690
it's a good idea to incorporate.

00:06:19.334 --> 00:06:23.866
All right so, at YC we strongly believe
that the US is the best place to

00:06:23.866 --> 00:06:28.481
incorporate a company, regardless
of where the founders come from or

00:06:28.481 --> 00:06:32.960
where they are when they're
actually incorporating.

00:06:32.960 --> 00:06:35.900
And the reason for this is that

00:06:35.900 --> 00:06:41.040
the vast majority of the capital that's
available to startups is here in the US.

00:06:41.040 --> 00:06:46.380
And, generally, US investors struggle
to invest in non-US companies.

00:06:46.380 --> 00:06:49.100
There are some exceptions and,
it is possible, but

00:06:49.100 --> 00:06:52.260
generally it causes problems.

00:06:52.260 --> 00:06:57.070
And even for, when you are going
through the process of incorporation,

00:06:57.070 --> 00:07:00.200
you don't actually have to be physically
in the US whilst you are incorporating.

00:07:03.020 --> 00:07:08.410
So then in the US, you can incorporate
your company in any of the 50 states.

00:07:08.410 --> 00:07:11.190
You don't have to, again,
be physically located in that state.

00:07:11.190 --> 00:07:15.160
You don't have to be planning to
do any business in that state.

00:07:16.240 --> 00:07:19.750
And actually, for startups, and
for a lot of companies actually,

00:07:19.750 --> 00:07:23.310
the state that most startups
will incorporate in is Delaware.

00:07:25.760 --> 00:07:31.440
And the reason being is that
Delaware law is highly developed.

00:07:31.440 --> 00:07:36.950
Pretty much all lawyers who are working on
corporate law in the US know Delaware law.

00:07:36.950 --> 00:07:39.950
Investors expect to see
Delaware companies.

00:07:39.950 --> 00:07:44.890
Delaware law allows companies the most
flexibility in terms of issuing shares and

00:07:44.890 --> 00:07:45.880
various other things.

00:07:45.880 --> 00:07:49.140
And also has the best privacy
protections for you, as owners.

00:07:49.140 --> 00:07:51.770
So it makes a lot of sense
to incorporate in Delaware.

00:07:51.770 --> 00:07:55.100
Because, again, this is one of these
situations where there's no point trying

00:07:55.100 --> 00:07:56.670
to do something different.

00:07:56.670 --> 00:07:59.180
Just go with the herd on this.

00:08:01.430 --> 00:08:05.220
And again you can incorporate
in Delaware as non US citizens.

00:08:05.220 --> 00:08:09.050
You can incorporate in Delaware when
you're not physically in the US.

00:08:09.050 --> 00:08:10.430
There's no problems there.

00:08:14.002 --> 00:08:18.120
All right, so let's move on to
actually how we incorporate.

00:08:18.120 --> 00:08:23.348
So, it's a two step process, and
so the first part is simply faxing

00:08:23.348 --> 00:08:28.965
a couple of forms into Delaware to say,
I would like to create an entity.

00:08:28.965 --> 00:08:34.060
And that takes about 24 hours for
them to process and get the entity formed.

00:08:35.420 --> 00:08:40.370
Once that entity has been formed,
then the second step in the process is to

00:08:40.370 --> 00:08:45.310
adopt bylaws for the company which
talk about how the company's

00:08:45.310 --> 00:08:50.800
going to be governed, create a board,
appoint directors to that board,

00:08:50.800 --> 00:08:55.720
appoint officers,
to assign IP to the company so you,

00:08:55.720 --> 00:09:00.320
as individuals, anything you start
creating is owned by the company and

00:09:00.320 --> 00:09:05.930
also to give out shares in the company,
so to assign ownership in the company.

00:09:09.110 --> 00:09:11.190
The final couple of those,
so assigning an IP and

00:09:11.190 --> 00:09:13.570
buying shares I'm going to talk
about in a little bit more detail.

00:09:13.570 --> 00:09:18.930
The other three are very legal and
complex, so I won't go into those here.

00:09:18.930 --> 00:09:21.530
But suffice it to say there is

00:09:21.530 --> 00:09:24.220
a couple of different options
that you can use to do this.

00:09:24.220 --> 00:09:27.280
And the first one is to just use a lawyer.

00:09:27.280 --> 00:09:30.580
You know, a lot of companies will
just hire a lawyer to do this.

00:09:30.580 --> 00:09:35.330
And that's a great option, assuming
especially if you want to do something

00:09:35.330 --> 00:09:40.530
a little bit non standard in some way or
you need some kind of high touch service.

00:09:41.920 --> 00:09:47.410
And it usually costs in
the region of $3,000 to $5,000

00:09:47.410 --> 00:09:53.340
plus filing fees to incorporate
a company with a lawyer.

00:09:53.340 --> 00:09:57.110
There's loads of lawyers here in
the valley, and most of those for

00:09:57.110 --> 00:10:00.280
startups will actually defer
their fees until you raise money.

00:10:00.280 --> 00:10:02.650
So often those fees can be pushed back,
and

00:10:02.650 --> 00:10:04.010
you don't have to pay those immediately.

00:10:05.860 --> 00:10:10.623
However what doesn't work with
lawyers is if you have a friend or

00:10:10.623 --> 00:10:15.648
a cousin who lives in Alabama and
is a real estate property lawyer and

00:10:15.648 --> 00:10:19.105
having them do
the incorporation documents.

00:10:19.105 --> 00:10:23.377
Because they don't understand how
startups work, they don't understand what

00:10:23.377 --> 00:10:26.518
the standard things are that
startup documents include and

00:10:26.518 --> 00:10:28.770
so things can go wrong.

00:10:28.770 --> 00:10:35.130
An example of this is, we had a company
that came to us in YC that we funded and

00:10:35.130 --> 00:10:39.710
they came to us as an LLC
formed in Connecticut.

00:10:41.200 --> 00:10:44.530
And the reason why they'd done
that is because the founders'

00:10:44.530 --> 00:10:46.760
friends who were lawyers in
Connecticut told them to do that.

00:10:47.780 --> 00:10:51.860
And so when we said we were going to
invest they decided that they were going

00:10:51.860 --> 00:10:55.960
to convert this at that point
to a Delaware C corp and

00:10:55.960 --> 00:11:00.910
they used their Connecticut lawyers
to do this, which is great.

00:11:00.910 --> 00:11:05.600
However, three funding rounds later,
when the company was

00:11:05.600 --> 00:11:10.510
raising a huge amount of money, a new law
firm that they'd hired here in the valley,

00:11:10.510 --> 00:11:13.910
actually discovered that that
conversion had been done wrong

00:11:13.910 --> 00:11:16.805
by the Connecticut lawyers, and
therefore it was not valid.

00:11:16.805 --> 00:11:20.675
They'd made a very simple mistake but
as a result it wasn't valid and so

00:11:20.675 --> 00:11:22.905
they had to put the funding
round on hold and

00:11:22.905 --> 00:11:25.545
had to sort this out before
they could raise the money.

00:11:25.545 --> 00:11:29.705
It took them six months,
it involved four different law firms and

00:11:29.705 --> 00:11:31.162
it cost them half a million dollars.

00:11:32.590 --> 00:11:37.790
So the moral of the story is use valley
lawyers if you're going to do this, and

00:11:37.790 --> 00:11:38.620
start with a Delaware C corp.

00:11:41.170 --> 00:11:43.680
Another option that's
really great especially for

00:11:43.680 --> 00:11:47.040
young start ups when
they're forming is Clerky.

00:11:47.040 --> 00:11:53.050
Clerky is a YC company and they have
a platform that allows you to create

00:11:53.050 --> 00:11:57.520
all of these formation documents using
a very simple interface where you plug in

00:11:57.520 --> 00:12:01.990
a bunch of details and all the forms get
populated with those information, so

00:12:01.990 --> 00:12:04.160
everything is internally consistent.

00:12:04.160 --> 00:12:07.720
And they allow everything to be
e-signed on their platform, and

00:12:07.720 --> 00:12:09.810
then they store it all for you.

00:12:09.810 --> 00:12:13.040
So this is a really great option if
you're just going with the basics,

00:12:13.040 --> 00:12:14.090
you just want to get it done.

00:12:14.090 --> 00:12:18.710
It costs a few hundred dollars,
again, plus filing fees.

00:12:18.710 --> 00:12:22.060
So it's a much more
straightforward process.

00:12:22.060 --> 00:12:26.150
And when we have companies who come to
us at YC who we have agreed to fund who

00:12:26.150 --> 00:12:28.960
haven't been incorporated yet,
that's where we send them.

00:12:28.960 --> 00:12:30.910
We tell them to go and
incorporate on Clerky.

00:12:34.685 --> 00:12:39.710
Okay, so part of the incorporation
process then is assigning equity.

00:12:40.840 --> 00:12:47.870
And, this is a really important process,
because one of the things that happens is,

00:12:47.870 --> 00:12:51.050
it actually creates a conversation
between the founders.

00:12:52.320 --> 00:12:56.310
It's really important that you do have
that discussion amongst the founders to

00:12:56.310 --> 00:12:59.190
understand how this is going to work.

00:12:59.190 --> 00:13:03.880
This can often ferret
out issues early around,

00:13:03.880 --> 00:13:06.190
is one founder expecting
to work on this part time?

00:13:06.190 --> 00:13:08.570
Is one founder expecting
to work on this full time?

00:13:08.570 --> 00:13:13.331
It can just try and pull out some issues
that might potentially become big problems

00:13:13.331 --> 00:13:14.228
down the road.

00:13:14.228 --> 00:13:18.491
And of course, the shares that you
own in the company as a founder,

00:13:18.491 --> 00:13:20.471
if the company is successful,

00:13:20.471 --> 00:13:25.290
it's likely to be the most significant
source of wealth creation for you.

00:13:25.290 --> 00:13:28.350
So it's important that you do
actually get this right and

00:13:28.350 --> 00:13:30.960
that all founders feel its fair.

00:13:30.960 --> 00:13:34.320
Fairness is a really
important idea here because

00:13:34.320 --> 00:13:37.830
you're going to be working with
your co-founders for a long time.

00:13:37.830 --> 00:13:42.670
You're going to be working in a very
stressful environment and if you have

00:13:42.670 --> 00:13:46.970
a situation where there's one founder
who has 90% of the shares and

00:13:46.970 --> 00:13:51.220
another founder who has 10% of the shares
but they're both pouring everything

00:13:51.220 --> 00:13:55.330
they have into this company to make it
a success, resentment can build up.

00:13:55.330 --> 00:13:58.687
And in a stressful situation the
resentment can bubble up and become worse

00:13:58.687 --> 00:14:02.790
and worse and it's the number one reason
that we find co-founders break up

00:14:02.790 --> 00:14:08.170
because they just can't get to the point
where this makes sense to them anymore.

00:14:08.170 --> 00:14:10.962
And actually in a lot of
these situations as well,

00:14:10.962 --> 00:14:16.405
when a co-founder dispute happens
around this, so much effort and

00:14:16.405 --> 00:14:21.295
time is poured into it that actually,
the company struggles to survive.

00:14:21.295 --> 00:14:25.395
It struggles to turn itself around after
the founders have figured this out.

00:14:25.395 --> 00:14:29.015
One of them's departed and
it's all been somewhat acrimonious.

00:14:29.015 --> 00:14:31.005
So have these conversations upfront.

00:14:31.005 --> 00:14:32.325
Make sure you understand.

00:14:32.325 --> 00:14:34.015
Make sure everybody feels it's fair.

00:14:35.110 --> 00:14:37.850
And in general,
we think that equity should be more or

00:14:37.850 --> 00:14:40.890
less split evenly amongst co-founders.

00:14:40.890 --> 00:14:43.140
And I'm talking about more or
less, it doesn't have to be exact.

00:14:44.400 --> 00:14:47.875
But often,
founders will push back on us on this.

00:14:47.875 --> 00:14:49.610
And some of the the examples that they or

00:14:49.610 --> 00:14:52.770
some of the reasons they will
give to us for saying no,

00:14:52.770 --> 00:14:57.340
no I should have 70% of the equity and my
co-founder should have 30% of the equity,

00:14:57.340 --> 00:15:02.640
is because I thought of the idea or
I built the prototype or

00:15:02.640 --> 00:15:07.529
I closed the first 20k in sales or I
started three months before my co-founder.

00:15:08.930 --> 00:15:14.420
And our responses to this is
everything is ahead of you.

00:15:14.420 --> 00:15:19.380
This is the early days of the company and
if you've worked there for three months.

00:15:19.380 --> 00:15:20.788
You've probably got 5, 10,

00:15:20.788 --> 00:15:25.220
15 years of this company ahead of
you if it's going to be a success.

00:15:25.220 --> 00:15:29.761
So think about forwards not backwards
all the efforts are in front of you.

00:15:29.761 --> 00:15:34.721
You're going to be iterating on
the products, the prototype is probably

00:15:34.721 --> 00:15:40.300
going to bear no resemblance to the final
product that becomes successful.

00:15:40.300 --> 00:15:44.306
It's all ahead of you and the time
commitments, again, it's 10 years,

00:15:44.306 --> 00:15:45.000
15 years.

00:15:45.000 --> 00:15:49.140
It's a long time, so
three months is just a drop in the ocean.

00:15:50.210 --> 00:15:55.430
The only one that maybe is
somewhat more of an argument to

00:15:55.430 --> 00:16:00.280
have slightly more, I'm not talking about
significantly more, is you might want to

00:16:00.280 --> 00:16:04.790
have one co-founder who has slightly more
shares just to prevent founder deadlock.

00:16:04.790 --> 00:16:06.423
In situations.

00:16:09.754 --> 00:16:13.719
But to be honest, if you're at the point
where the founders are having to vote

00:16:13.719 --> 00:16:15.428
their shares to make decisions,

00:16:15.428 --> 00:16:19.761
then there's probably something more
fundamentally broken in the relationship.

00:16:21.180 --> 00:16:22.460
This is not going to solve that.

00:16:23.530 --> 00:16:26.588
So in conclusion, when you're
thinking about allocating equity,

00:16:26.588 --> 00:16:30.343
think about everything that's ahead of
you not everything that you've just done.

00:16:33.620 --> 00:16:37.716
So you discussed it, you will decide
it's fair, you've all got more or

00:16:37.716 --> 00:16:39.620
less even stock splits.

00:16:39.620 --> 00:16:46.356
You do actually have to do some
paperwork to make this actually happen.

00:16:46.356 --> 00:16:50.380
And again, this is the kind of
thing that can cause problems.

00:16:50.380 --> 00:16:55.549
Again, another company that we invested
in came to us already incorporated,

00:16:55.549 --> 00:16:59.716
they already had all of their shares and
everything all set up.

00:16:59.716 --> 00:17:03.799
But for various reasons they actually
had to incorporate a new company.

00:17:03.799 --> 00:17:08.140
And merged the old company
into the new company.

00:17:08.140 --> 00:17:13.005
The shares that they got in,
they basically swapped the shares in

00:17:13.005 --> 00:17:16.570
the new company for
shares in the old company.

00:17:16.570 --> 00:17:19.600
Carried on, did well, raised some money.

00:17:19.600 --> 00:17:23.550
Then raised another round of money with,
again, new lawyers.

00:17:23.550 --> 00:17:26.532
New lawyers always go through all
the paperwork with a fine tooth comb.

00:17:26.532 --> 00:17:31.810
And the new lawyers discovered this
actually in the original company.

00:17:31.810 --> 00:17:34.330
They had never papered
the share purchases.

00:17:34.330 --> 00:17:37.720
So they didn't actually legally own
the shares which meant that then,

00:17:37.720 --> 00:17:40.598
when they swapped them for
the new shares in the new company.

00:17:40.598 --> 00:17:42.891
They didn't own the new
shares in the new company,

00:17:42.891 --> 00:17:45.570
because they hadn't actually
done a two way transaction.

00:17:45.570 --> 00:17:48.790
They hadn't given one thing in return for
something else.

00:17:48.790 --> 00:17:52.490
And so everything they've done
since as shareholders were invalid.

00:17:52.490 --> 00:17:54.520
And they didn't have part of the company.

00:17:54.520 --> 00:17:59.030
So again, cue lots of lawyers,
lots of time, lots of money.

00:17:59.030 --> 00:18:03.050
And it's just not what needs to
be focused on by the company.

00:18:05.390 --> 00:18:10.050
So the way that you buy your shares as
founders is on a stock purchase agreement.

00:18:12.100 --> 00:18:16.560
And as I say,
you exchange it usually for cash.

00:18:16.560 --> 00:18:21.280
So in the case of a formation, you usually
pay a few dollars to buy your shares.

00:18:21.280 --> 00:18:24.770
And the money gets deposited from
your individual bank account

00:18:24.770 --> 00:18:26.570
into the company bank account.

00:18:26.570 --> 00:18:30.579
Sometimes it's in return for IP, but
that gets a little bit more complex.

00:18:33.296 --> 00:18:38.525
And then once you've bought your shares,
you have ownership of the company.

00:18:38.525 --> 00:18:40.625
And this is how you record the ownership.

00:18:40.625 --> 00:18:42.460
You use a cap table.

00:18:42.460 --> 00:18:45.360
Now, this is a really basic cap table.

00:18:45.360 --> 00:18:48.131
It will get more complex
as the company goes on.

00:18:48.131 --> 00:18:52.288
You'll issue shares to employees,
you'll issue shares to investors.

00:18:52.288 --> 00:18:56.802
But basically the key points of this are
that you're recording how many shares each

00:18:56.802 --> 00:18:58.880
of the people on the cap table are own.

00:18:58.880 --> 00:19:03.160
So in this case currently the founders,
and what their ownership is.

00:19:03.160 --> 00:19:05.710
So in this example,
we have three founders.

00:19:05.710 --> 00:19:07.889
They each have same number of shares, and

00:19:07.889 --> 00:19:10.519
they each have the same
ownership of the company.

00:19:10.519 --> 00:19:14.277
I'll come back to this cap table so
get build on it as we get through here.

00:19:19.288 --> 00:19:26.429
So one of the, Key parts of the stock
purchase agreement that you will sign

00:19:26.429 --> 00:19:30.795
is as founders is that there are actually
some restrictions on these shares.

00:19:30.795 --> 00:19:34.730
So you own them as of
the day you buy them.

00:19:34.730 --> 00:19:38.140
But actually if a founder was going to

00:19:38.140 --> 00:19:43.280
leave then the company has the right to
take some of those shares back over time.

00:19:43.280 --> 00:19:47.140
And as time progresses the number of
shares that the company has the right to

00:19:47.140 --> 00:19:48.890
repurchase reduces.

00:19:48.890 --> 00:19:50.880
And that's called vesting.

00:19:50.880 --> 00:19:52.380
So to put it in the opposite way around,

00:19:52.380 --> 00:19:56.890
vesting is earning the right to permanent
ownership of the shares over time.

00:19:58.370 --> 00:20:00.720
And here for startups, again,

00:20:00.720 --> 00:20:06.000
it makes sense to use the standard
template, the standard process.

00:20:06.000 --> 00:20:09.689
And generally, that is four year
vesting with a one year cliff.

00:20:10.820 --> 00:20:16.760
And what that means is if you look at this
graph over here, we've got time along

00:20:16.760 --> 00:20:22.900
the bottom and we've got the percentage of
your shares that's invested on the y axis.

00:20:22.900 --> 00:20:24.896
And so the cliff happens at 1 year.

00:20:24.896 --> 00:20:27.597
So between day 1 and day 365,

00:20:27.597 --> 00:20:32.242
all of your shares are subject
to the repurchase option.

00:20:32.242 --> 00:20:37.141
But then on day 365,
25% of them are immediately released, so

00:20:37.141 --> 00:20:39.594
that's where the cliff comes in.

00:20:39.594 --> 00:20:44.778
Then, after that, each month of the year,
148 of your shares will vest.

00:20:44.778 --> 00:20:47.945
So that they're gradually
being released so

00:20:47.945 --> 00:20:52.793
that by the end of the four years,
100% of the shares are vested.

00:20:52.793 --> 00:20:56.835
So basically, depending on where
you are on the time period of this,

00:20:56.835 --> 00:20:58.786
if you were to leave the company,

00:20:58.786 --> 00:21:03.127
you would be able to calculate how many
shares you would keep as a founder.

00:21:03.127 --> 00:21:06.300
And how many shares would be
repurchased by the company.

00:21:07.430 --> 00:21:10.680
And the documents are written in such
a way that the shares that get repurchased

00:21:10.680 --> 00:21:14.700
by the company are purchased at the price
that was originally paid for them.

00:21:14.700 --> 00:21:17.645
So there's no gain or
anything built into that.

00:21:17.645 --> 00:21:25.740
The reason why we have vesting is there's
actually a number of different reasons.

00:21:27.410 --> 00:21:33.830
So the first one is because it provides
protection to any remaining founders.

00:21:33.830 --> 00:21:39.520
So if you think of an example where you
are working on a company where there's

00:21:39.520 --> 00:21:42.900
three of you in total, so
you and two co-founders.

00:21:42.900 --> 00:21:46.512
Now let's assume that one of your
co-founders, and you don't have vesting.

00:21:46.512 --> 00:21:50.023
Let's assume one of your
co-founders leaves in six months.

00:21:50.023 --> 00:21:54.400
But the remaining two founders carry on
working on this company, put a huge amount

00:21:54.400 --> 00:21:58.471
of effort in, build up a successful
company over the next five to ten years.

00:21:58.471 --> 00:22:02.820
Meanwhile, the ex-founder is sitting
on a beach, drinking cocktails.

00:22:02.820 --> 00:22:07.633
Doing all these things that
people have time to do.

00:22:07.633 --> 00:22:08.693
How do you feel?

00:22:08.693 --> 00:22:12.378
You're not going to feel massively happy
that this person has the same amount of

00:22:12.378 --> 00:22:14.997
value, and
you're creating all this value for them.

00:22:14.997 --> 00:22:18.055
And then think about if
your company gets acquired.

00:22:18.055 --> 00:22:22.166
Let's say your company is acquired for
$500 million.

00:22:22.166 --> 00:22:24.995
Without vesting,
that ex-cofounder would get

00:22:24.995 --> 00:22:28.500
the same amount of money as
the two remaining founders.

00:22:28.500 --> 00:22:30.360
Which again,
is not going to make you feel that great.

00:22:31.540 --> 00:22:33.906
So it protects for most situations.

00:22:33.906 --> 00:22:37.742
If there had been vesting and
that founder had left after six months,

00:22:37.742 --> 00:22:40.203
then they would have
had no shares in there.

00:22:40.203 --> 00:22:44.332
And the remaining founders who put in all
the effort over the next five to ten years

00:22:44.332 --> 00:22:46.530
would have seen all of the value creation.

00:22:49.070 --> 00:22:52.340
Somewhat linked to that is the concept
of having skin in the game.

00:22:52.340 --> 00:22:57.274
So this creates an incentive to actually
work hard on the company because you

00:22:57.274 --> 00:23:01.454
need to be there for a long time
to actually keep all your shares.

00:23:01.454 --> 00:23:07.360
And this is something again that
investors care deeply about.

00:23:07.360 --> 00:23:11.055
Because they don't want to put in a bunch
of money into a company when really

00:23:11.055 --> 00:23:12.945
they're investing in the founders.

00:23:12.945 --> 00:23:15.436
Because they're the people who
are going to actually make this happen.

00:23:15.436 --> 00:23:18.630
But the founders turn around after
a month and say, yeah, we're out.

00:23:18.630 --> 00:23:21.400
We're going to get some new
people in to run the company.

00:23:21.400 --> 00:23:23.340
So it protects the investors as well.

00:23:25.150 --> 00:23:30.498
And finally, the final reason is that
it sets an example to future employees.

00:23:30.498 --> 00:23:36.280
And as we'll see in a little while, you
would expect employees to having vesting.

00:23:36.280 --> 00:23:40.926
And so it's very unfair to say to your
employees you must have vesting, but

00:23:40.926 --> 00:23:42.340
I don't as a founder.

00:23:42.340 --> 00:23:47.736
So it suggests a good way to be,
we all have the same situation here.

00:23:53.091 --> 00:23:54.840
Okay, final point on vesting.

00:23:54.840 --> 00:23:57.620
And this is an important one when you
come around to do your paperwork.

00:23:59.180 --> 00:24:03.220
There is one piece of paper as part
of the incorporation process called

00:24:03.220 --> 00:24:04.740
an 83b election.

00:24:04.740 --> 00:24:08.550
And basically, I'm not going to go
into a huge amount of details, but

00:24:08.550 --> 00:24:10.661
basically if you Don't sign it.

00:24:10.661 --> 00:24:13.945
Then you're taxed on
the increase in value in your

00:24:13.945 --> 00:24:16.379
shares every time your shares vest.

00:24:16.379 --> 00:24:20.116
So if you think about,
that graph is after the past year, and

00:24:20.116 --> 00:24:22.840
then every month over
the next three years.

00:24:24.060 --> 00:24:27.920
So this can create huge
personal tax liabilities.

00:24:27.920 --> 00:24:32.230
And it can also,
it also creates company tax liabilities.

00:24:32.230 --> 00:24:33.120
So this is a bad thing.

00:24:34.500 --> 00:24:38.720
So the way to solve this is to
file your 83B election, and

00:24:38.720 --> 00:24:43.080
basically then you don't have this
taxation over the vesting period.

00:24:44.290 --> 00:24:49.780
It’s the one thing in incorporation
that cannot be fixed if you don't do it.

00:24:49.780 --> 00:24:52.977
So you have to file it with
the IRS within 30 days.

00:24:52.977 --> 00:24:55.105
And it's best to retain proof of mailing,
so

00:24:55.105 --> 00:24:57.241
that you can actually
show that you did that.

00:24:57.241 --> 00:25:02.551
If you get down the line in an acquisition
or when you're doing funding round,

00:25:02.551 --> 00:25:07.390
it will get asked for, that proof will
get asked for that you signed it.

00:25:08.430 --> 00:25:14.480
And we have seen deals fall apart
over this, both funding rounds and

00:25:14.480 --> 00:25:18.930
acquisition deals, because the investor or
the acquirer has been spooked by

00:25:18.930 --> 00:25:22.620
the potential liabilities on the company,
and they just don't want to go there.

00:25:22.620 --> 00:25:23.840
And they walk away from the deal.

00:25:24.950 --> 00:25:28.610
So the way to solve this,
is follow the instructions,

00:25:28.610 --> 00:25:32.030
either when you're dealing with lawyers or
when you're dealing with Clerky.

00:25:32.030 --> 00:25:35.080
They will give you instructions
about how to complete the form.

00:25:35.080 --> 00:25:38.370
They will give you instructions about
where to send it, how to do it.

00:25:38.370 --> 00:25:42.314
Just follow those instructions,
and all will be fine.

00:25:46.575 --> 00:25:48.370
All right, let's move on to fundraising.

00:25:48.370 --> 00:25:52.300
So you've now got a company that
can actually raise some money.

00:25:54.220 --> 00:25:57.760
And this is probably something that

00:25:57.760 --> 00:26:00.790
you'll hear a lot about over
the course of the rest of this course.

00:26:00.790 --> 00:26:05.540
So I'm not going to talk here
about the strategy around pricing,

00:26:05.540 --> 00:26:08.060
or valuation, or
how you talk to investors.

00:26:08.060 --> 00:26:11.919
I'm talking more about the sort of nuts
and bolts that happen behind the scenes.

00:26:14.540 --> 00:26:18.970
In very simple terms, you can either raise
money on a priced round, or you can also

00:26:18.970 --> 00:26:24.090
raise money on a non-priced round, which
are also known as convertible rounds.

00:26:24.090 --> 00:26:26.532
So in a priced round,
the shares are sold for

00:26:26.532 --> 00:26:28.910
a specific price at
the time of that round.

00:26:28.910 --> 00:26:33.774
And in a non-priced or convertible round,
the investor gives money now, but

00:26:33.774 --> 00:26:35.995
the shares are given in the future.

00:26:35.995 --> 00:26:39.131
And the ordering for this,
usually in the US, and

00:26:39.131 --> 00:26:43.088
usually in the valley is that
startups will first raise money on

00:26:43.088 --> 00:26:46.090
a unpriced round to raise
a little bit of money.

00:26:46.090 --> 00:26:51.957
And then a couple of years later, they'll
hopefully raise money on a price strand.

00:26:51.957 --> 00:26:57.380
The rest of the world hasn't quite caught
up to this idea of unpriced rounds.

00:26:57.380 --> 00:27:01.224
So generally, we see when companies
are coming us from around the world, and

00:27:01.224 --> 00:27:04.140
they actually raise money
immediately on priced rounds.

00:27:06.410 --> 00:27:10.940
So let's go through an example of a priced
round, and you can see how this works.

00:27:10.940 --> 00:27:12.510
So other say,

00:27:12.510 --> 00:27:18.360
the valuation of the rounds sets the price
that the investor will pay to buy a share.

00:27:19.560 --> 00:27:22.820
So let's work through this,
and with an example.

00:27:22.820 --> 00:27:25.769
So let's assume we have
the same companies before.

00:27:27.130 --> 00:27:31.170
Our founders have 9 million shares,
and they've done really well, and

00:27:31.170 --> 00:27:35.333
they're going to raise $2 million
at an $8 million valuation.

00:27:35.333 --> 00:27:39.855
Now, that $8 million valuation
is premoney, is the terminology.

00:27:39.855 --> 00:27:42.917
So that basically means before
the investor puts any money in,

00:27:42.917 --> 00:27:45.070
the valuation of the company is 8 million.

00:27:46.140 --> 00:27:49.910
And so the way that you calculate
the price for the shares that the investor

00:27:49.910 --> 00:27:54.900
will buy, is you just divide the 8
million valuation by the premoney shares,

00:27:54.900 --> 00:27:57.950
so the 9 million shares,
which gives a price of 89 cents.

00:27:59.390 --> 00:28:06.676
And so then they will be able to buy
2.25 million shares for that $2 million.

00:28:06.676 --> 00:28:10.607
The post money valuation at
this point is $10 million.

00:28:10.607 --> 00:28:14.543
Because it was the $8 million valuation
before the investor brought money in, and

00:28:14.543 --> 00:28:17.270
now this $2 million sitting
in the bank account as well.

00:28:17.270 --> 00:28:19.850
So it's increased the value of
the company up by 2 million, so

00:28:19.850 --> 00:28:21.175
the post money is 10 million.

00:28:21.175 --> 00:28:24.790
And just you'll hear those terminologies
just to get them straight in your mind.

00:28:26.480 --> 00:28:28.990
So now,
this is what the cap table looks like.

00:28:28.990 --> 00:28:35.510
So we still have our three founders,
they still own 3 million shares each.

00:28:35.510 --> 00:28:38.450
But we've now created
a new class of shares

00:28:38.450 --> 00:28:41.520
which have been sold to the investor.

00:28:41.520 --> 00:28:43.850
And now,
the investor owns 20% of the company.

00:28:45.490 --> 00:28:49.766
Each of the founders have
been reduced from 33% of

00:28:49.766 --> 00:28:52.881
the company to 26% of the company.

00:28:52.881 --> 00:28:58.355
Now, this makes it all sound really
simple and actually, it's not.

00:28:58.355 --> 00:29:01.915
There's various other complexities in
here that I'm not going to talk about.

00:29:01.915 --> 00:29:05.401
And there's a lot of documents that
are involved in here with a lot of

00:29:05.401 --> 00:29:08.085
things that need to be negotiated.

00:29:08.085 --> 00:29:13.535
So if a company is doing a priced round,
then you would

00:29:13.535 --> 00:29:17.345
need to use a lawyer, you need to hire
a lawyer to help you with all of that.

00:29:17.345 --> 00:29:20.002
And they'd be able to talk you
through the whole process.

00:29:24.638 --> 00:29:29.731
So moving on to the unpriced rounds,
and for most of companies who raised

00:29:29.731 --> 00:29:35.324
money now out here in the Bay Area, is
they will use an instrument called a SAFE,

00:29:35.324 --> 00:29:39.190
which stands for
Simple Agreement for Future Equity.

00:29:40.320 --> 00:29:43.870
And the simple is really important
because it's actually is simple.

00:29:43.870 --> 00:29:47.290
It's only a few pages long,
so it's easy to understand.

00:29:47.290 --> 00:29:51.310
We also have this documents on
the White Combinator websites and

00:29:51.310 --> 00:29:55.340
really great primer,
this explains a lot more about them.

00:29:55.340 --> 00:29:57.639
So you can look at that for
some more details.

00:29:58.740 --> 00:30:04.018
And basically what it's saying is
the investor is giving the company

00:30:04.018 --> 00:30:09.301
money now in return for the right to
receive shares in a future round.

00:30:09.301 --> 00:30:12.934
But the investor's giving the money now,
and so they don't want to receive

00:30:12.934 --> 00:30:15.678
shares in a future round at
the price of the future round.

00:30:15.678 --> 00:30:18.803
Because when they invest now, they're
probably investing at a much riskier

00:30:18.803 --> 00:30:20.398
stage, it's a much earlier startup.

00:30:20.398 --> 00:30:24.500
And so they want some kind of bonus or
deal to reflect that.

00:30:25.650 --> 00:30:28.738
And so the way that, that's built in is,

00:30:28.738 --> 00:30:32.635
the safe includes what's
called an valuation cap.

00:30:32.635 --> 00:30:36.330
And what this cap does,
is it sets a valuation upper bound for

00:30:36.330 --> 00:30:39.822
the calculation of working
out the price for the shares.

00:30:42.173 --> 00:30:46.350
So again, as an example,
we'll use our same company.

00:30:46.350 --> 00:30:51.291
9 million shares for the founders,
but this time in April of this year,

00:30:51.291 --> 00:30:55.592
they raise $400,000 on SAFEs
with a $4 million cap.

00:30:55.592 --> 00:31:00.393
And then fast forward some time,
we're going to the future, December 2017,

00:31:00.393 --> 00:31:04.870
they raise a $2 million round,
that's an 8 million premoney valuation.

00:31:04.870 --> 00:31:08.970
So this time, there's actually two
calculations that need to happen,

00:31:08.970 --> 00:31:14.200
because there's the SAFE conversion price
and there's the priced round price.

00:31:14.200 --> 00:31:18.250
And you can see here that
the SAFE conversion price uses

00:31:18.250 --> 00:31:21.370
the $4 million cap as the valuation.

00:31:21.370 --> 00:31:25.912
Whereas, the round price uses
the $8 million valuation,

00:31:25.912 --> 00:31:31.268
even though all these shares are being
actually given at the same time.

00:31:31.268 --> 00:31:36.318
And so as a result,
the SAFE holder here has a price per share

00:31:36.318 --> 00:31:41.480
of half the series A investors,
the price round investors.

00:31:42.560 --> 00:31:44.450
So that means that actually for
their money,

00:31:44.450 --> 00:31:47.870
because they came in early,
they're getting twice as many shares

00:31:47.870 --> 00:31:51.720
as somebody who was putting in the same
amount of money in the priced round.

00:31:51.720 --> 00:31:55.210
So that's how they get their
sort of bonus in all of this.

00:31:59.150 --> 00:32:03.150
So again, the cap table, by the time
we get to the December priced round,

00:32:03.150 --> 00:32:05.290
the cap table looks like this.

00:32:05.290 --> 00:32:09.317
So now, we also have the SAFE Investor
in there on the cap table.

00:32:09.317 --> 00:32:12.499
And they own the same class of shares.

00:32:12.499 --> 00:32:13.603
Yes, question.

00:32:13.603 --> 00:32:19.461
&gt;&gt; Does a discount on the SAFE only kick
in if the money raised is below the cap?

00:32:19.461 --> 00:32:24.360
&gt;&gt; So most SAFEs are on a cap.

00:32:24.360 --> 00:32:29.240
There is a separate kind of
safe that has a discount.

00:32:29.240 --> 00:32:32.660
So are you asking does the cap kick in if

00:32:32.660 --> 00:32:35.030
the valuation of the round
is lower than the cap?

00:32:35.030 --> 00:32:38.360
&gt;&gt; So both of those aren't
applied in the same safe usually?

00:32:38.360 --> 00:32:39.130
&gt;&gt; Generally not.

00:32:39.130 --> 00:32:41.370
So generally you wouldn't have a cap and

00:32:41.370 --> 00:32:45.190
a discount because that's kind of
cherry picking for the investor.

00:32:45.190 --> 00:32:48.180
So you would try very hard
to avoid that situation.

00:32:48.180 --> 00:32:52.176
In the situation where you had a $4
million cap, but your round was at

00:32:52.176 --> 00:32:57.084
$3 million for instance, everybody would
just convert to the $3 million route.

00:33:00.628 --> 00:33:05.626
Okay, so
again we have the founders are now owning

00:33:05.626 --> 00:33:10.505
less of the company because
they've sold some of

00:33:10.505 --> 00:33:15.403
the company to their investors
&gt;&gt; Could you talk a little bit about

00:33:15.403 --> 00:33:18.765
why safe is preferred sometimes
sort of 10% [INAUDIBLE].

00:33:18.765 --> 00:33:24.805
&gt;&gt; Yes, so
the benefits of a safe over priced

00:33:24.805 --> 00:33:29.465
round is that it's a lot simpler, so
you can just sign one safe immediately and

00:33:29.465 --> 00:33:34.340
get the money, it doesn't require lawyers
&gt;&gt; And I'll come and

00:33:34.340 --> 00:33:35.200
do a few more details.

00:33:35.200 --> 00:33:36.970
But basically it's simple.

00:33:36.970 --> 00:33:38.180
And that's the important thing.

00:33:40.110 --> 00:33:42.200
Okay, so let's talk about dilution and

00:33:42.200 --> 00:33:44.770
then we'll talk a little
bit more about that.

00:33:44.770 --> 00:33:47.150
So the idea is this is the early
stage of your company.

00:33:47.150 --> 00:33:49.330
If you think about your company as a pie.

00:33:50.380 --> 00:33:53.350
Where the size of the pie is
the value of the company.

00:33:53.350 --> 00:33:58.470
In the early days, you have
a massive slice of a very small pie.

00:33:58.470 --> 00:34:02.100
And then as the company grows,
the size of the pie grows but

00:34:02.100 --> 00:34:03.540
your slice becomes smaller.

00:34:03.540 --> 00:34:06.890
Because you're giving away or
you're selling parts of the company.

00:34:06.890 --> 00:34:10.650
But the important thing here is
that actually your wealth and

00:34:10.650 --> 00:34:16.610
all of this is the area of your pie,
the area of your slice rather.

00:34:16.610 --> 00:34:19.640
So the thing to bear in mind is
that dilution is inevitable.

00:34:19.640 --> 00:34:20.480
It's going to happen.

00:34:20.480 --> 00:34:25.150
But the important thing is is that you're
still creating wealth for yourself and for

00:34:25.150 --> 00:34:27.740
your investors, because that's
how they make money as well.

00:34:27.740 --> 00:34:32.640
But it's just very important to actually
consider dilution as your going through.

00:34:34.890 --> 00:34:40.010
One thing that we see founders
make mistake early on is that

00:34:40.010 --> 00:34:44.100
they sell too much of the company
early on to very low valuation.

00:34:44.100 --> 00:34:46.800
And really in the early days of a company,

00:34:46.800 --> 00:34:48.840
you're still trying to figure out
what products you're building.

00:34:48.840 --> 00:34:50.780
You're still trying to figure
out what you're going to do.

00:34:50.780 --> 00:34:53.490
So what do you need to spend the money on?

00:34:53.490 --> 00:34:56.830
Well, mostly money when you spend you
money when you've raised is on hiring.

00:34:56.830 --> 00:34:59.680
But if you're at that stage,
you probably don't want to hire.

00:34:59.680 --> 00:35:04.480
So why raise more money than you
need if you can wait six months or

00:35:04.480 --> 00:35:07.120
a year, and rate at a higher valuation.

00:35:07.120 --> 00:35:09.410
So just something to bear in mind,

00:35:09.410 --> 00:35:12.130
as a way to not sell too much of
the company in the early days.

00:35:14.290 --> 00:35:18.910
Okay, so in terms of the logistics as
I was saying, price round is complex,

00:35:18.910 --> 00:35:20.000
as SAFE is simple.

00:35:22.740 --> 00:35:25.716
Again, with SAFEs you can use Clerky.

00:35:25.716 --> 00:35:28.732
They have a fundraising product as well,
so

00:35:28.732 --> 00:35:33.700
they have the standard YC
templates built into their system.

00:35:33.700 --> 00:35:38.071
So you can just plug in the investor
details, plug in the amount of money,

00:35:38.071 --> 00:35:40.539
send it off to your investor to be signed,

00:35:40.539 --> 00:35:45.216
they get the bank wire details as well so
they can send the money to the company.

00:35:45.216 --> 00:35:48.532
Which is also an important point
you do actually want the money

00:35:48.532 --> 00:35:49.946
back from the investors.

00:35:49.946 --> 00:35:55.810
Is very easy to say, we've got investors
in and it's great our first question is.

00:35:55.810 --> 00:36:00.500
Have you got the money in the bank and
to fund the sale, no because some excuse,

00:36:00.500 --> 00:36:04.410
you got to keep working on your investors
until the money is in the bank.

00:36:06.850 --> 00:36:10.690
The other thing that's a slightly,
just a minor point here.

00:36:10.690 --> 00:36:13.340
Is because,
you're selling equity in the company.

00:36:13.340 --> 00:36:18.870
The company, will need to complete a board
consent for part of this even with a safe.

00:36:18.870 --> 00:36:22.650
And, that's a legal document
with a specific form.

00:36:22.650 --> 00:36:25.702
And it's not enough just for the founders
to [INAUDIBLE] each other and say,

00:36:25.702 --> 00:36:27.770
we're doing this right.

00:36:27.770 --> 00:36:30.070
And again Clerky will
help you with that or

00:36:30.070 --> 00:36:33.140
if you're using lawyers to do any
of this they'll with it as well.

00:36:33.140 --> 00:36:34.520
So it's just something to bear in mind.

00:36:37.780 --> 00:36:39.830
All right, so
now you've raised some money,

00:36:39.830 --> 00:36:41.900
what are you going to do with that money?

00:36:41.900 --> 00:36:44.899
Well probably you're going to spend
most of that money on hiring.

00:36:47.360 --> 00:36:50.920
And again, hiring is a complex area.

00:36:52.800 --> 00:36:55.490
It's governed by a lot of laws and
regulations.

00:36:55.490 --> 00:36:57.540
And I'm no specialist.

00:36:57.540 --> 00:37:00.220
Don't expect you as
founders to be specialists.

00:37:00.220 --> 00:37:03.601
The important thing is that you understand
the basics and that you understand that

00:37:03.601 --> 00:37:06.151
there are people out there who
could help you with all of this.

00:37:10.458 --> 00:37:14.846
Okay, so the first part of this is
deciding if you're going to hire somebody,

00:37:14.846 --> 00:37:16.650
what role they're going to be.

00:37:16.650 --> 00:37:23.900
So in the US, again, you can hire somebody
either as a contractor or as an employee.

00:37:23.900 --> 00:37:26.200
And they have slightly
different characteristics and

00:37:26.200 --> 00:37:28.630
slightly different results in
terms of how you pay them.

00:37:29.950 --> 00:37:32.440
So in both places, a contractor and

00:37:32.440 --> 00:37:36.140
an employee will assign the IP
that they create to the company.

00:37:36.140 --> 00:37:39.150
So a contractor will sign
a consulting agreement,

00:37:39.150 --> 00:37:41.800
an employee will sign an IP
assignment agreement.

00:37:43.500 --> 00:37:48.950
But then for the contractor,
there's some requirement for them.

00:37:48.950 --> 00:37:54.300
So generally, they set their own
work hours, they work on a specific

00:37:54.300 --> 00:37:59.900
project with an end goal that's very
defined, they use their own equipment and

00:37:59.900 --> 00:38:04.160
they're not really involved in any
day to day running of the company.

00:38:04.160 --> 00:38:09.600
So an example of this would be, you
hire a designer to design your website,

00:38:09.600 --> 00:38:14.110
and it's a month long project and
they deliver you a website.

00:38:14.110 --> 00:38:17.020
And you say great this
is just what we need.

00:38:17.020 --> 00:38:18.440
And off they go onto their next project.

00:38:20.090 --> 00:38:24.210
On the other hand, employees will be
tend to use company equipment, so

00:38:24.210 --> 00:38:28.160
the company generally will
provide them with a laptop.

00:38:28.160 --> 00:38:32.235
They will usually be working company
hours and in and the company location.

00:38:32.235 --> 00:38:34.675
Of course,
with startups that gets a little bit gray.

00:38:36.245 --> 00:38:37.405
There's more supervision.

00:38:37.405 --> 00:38:40.775
They have less autonomy
over their decisions

00:38:40.775 --> 00:38:43.666
about how they actually do their work.

00:38:44.967 --> 00:38:48.817
And importantly for the US, for
an employee to work in the US,

00:38:48.817 --> 00:38:54.017
they need to have work authorization
whereas a contractor you could hire

00:38:54.017 --> 00:38:56.167
remotely and
they wouldn't need work authorization.

00:38:58.967 --> 00:39:03.447
So for contractors, if you decide
that you are hiring a contractor,

00:39:03.447 --> 00:39:08.030
then you would agree in the contract
how you would actually pay them, and

00:39:08.030 --> 00:39:10.630
usually that how the time
on milestone based.

00:39:10.630 --> 00:39:14.380
So, milestone based would be in our
example, the web designer may be you

00:39:14.380 --> 00:39:17.900
provide 25% of the fees up front and
you provide the other 75% of

00:39:17.900 --> 00:39:22.260
the fees when the designer provides
the final design for the website.

00:39:22.260 --> 00:39:26.600
It's really straightforward for
the company to pay them because they don't

00:39:26.600 --> 00:39:30.520
have to worry about any taxes being
withheld or anything else like that.

00:39:30.520 --> 00:39:33.730
So you just pay the contractor and
then at the end of the year,

00:39:33.730 --> 00:39:38.090
the company will create a form
that's called a form 1099.

00:39:38.090 --> 00:39:42.160
Which you send to the contractor so
that they can include the income in their

00:39:42.160 --> 00:39:45.620
personal tax returns, and
also the company sends one to the IRS.

00:39:47.030 --> 00:39:50.540
Obviously, that's the US regulation, so
you have a company that's not in the US,

00:39:50.540 --> 00:39:52.730
then you wouldn't need to file a 1099.

00:39:52.730 --> 00:39:56.280
And generally if you're working with
a contractor who's not in the US,

00:39:56.280 --> 00:39:58.030
you wouldn't need to file a 1099 either,

00:39:58.030 --> 00:40:01.540
but things get a little bit more
complex there, so take advice.

00:40:03.618 --> 00:40:08.155
On the flip side, paying employees
is more complex for the company.

00:40:08.155 --> 00:40:13.681
So, the company pays their employees,
but the company withholds tax

00:40:13.681 --> 00:40:18.739
on the employees' behalf and
pays it over to various agencies,

00:40:18.739 --> 00:40:24.188
so IRS, to state agencies, and
sometimes to city agencies as well.

00:40:24.188 --> 00:40:26.458
So, there's a lot of
calculations in there.

00:40:26.458 --> 00:40:30.998
It gets very complex very fast if you have
employees all over in different states.

00:40:33.206 --> 00:40:36.255
And it's more of a headache for
the company.

00:40:36.255 --> 00:40:39.835
But the IRS prefer it because they
get their tax every time the employee

00:40:39.835 --> 00:40:40.450
gets paid.

00:40:42.140 --> 00:40:46.629
And then so what happens at the end of the
year is the company provides a form W2 to

00:40:46.629 --> 00:40:49.711
each of the employees and
then the employee uses that to

00:40:49.711 --> 00:40:53.128
show how much tax has already
been withheld on their behalf.

00:40:53.128 --> 00:40:54.664
And how much income they have.

00:40:56.950 --> 00:41:01.516
So, this is a situation where you
should absolutely not be the person

00:41:01.516 --> 00:41:03.330
calculating all of this.

00:41:03.330 --> 00:41:05.610
You should use a payroll service provider.

00:41:06.840 --> 00:41:07.920
It's totally worth the money.

00:41:07.920 --> 00:41:09.350
It's not very expensive, and

00:41:09.350 --> 00:41:12.190
they basically take all of
this hassle away from you.

00:41:12.190 --> 00:41:15.680
And the company that
a lot of YC startups use,

00:41:15.680 --> 00:41:18.520
who is a YC company themselves, is Gusto.

00:41:19.820 --> 00:41:21.830
And they just deal with all of this for
you.

00:41:21.830 --> 00:41:23.067
It just makes life a lot easier.

00:41:25.857 --> 00:41:31.482
The other thing to bear in mind is that
under California law, in particular,

00:41:31.482 --> 00:41:36.775
but other laws as well, employees
are required to be paid minimum wage.

00:41:36.775 --> 00:41:40.858
So, you can't have somebody working for
you for free and

00:41:40.858 --> 00:41:45.100
expecting them to be producing work for
the company.

00:41:45.100 --> 00:41:48.501
So, do just bear in mind that when you are
hiring people, you do need to pay them.

00:41:52.854 --> 00:41:59.730
The other part of a startup employee's
compensation package is made up of equity.

00:41:59.730 --> 00:42:04.425
And usually this is, again,
the part that is the way

00:42:04.425 --> 00:42:09.459
that they possibly create more wealth for
themselves.

00:42:09.459 --> 00:42:13.489
It's really important that startup
founders do give employees equity,

00:42:13.489 --> 00:42:17.584
because it helps to incentivize everybody
to work towards the same thing,

00:42:17.584 --> 00:42:19.866
which is creating a successful company.

00:42:19.866 --> 00:42:24.178
And often in the early days, for employees
they're being paid below market rate,

00:42:24.178 --> 00:42:26.840
still above minimum wage,
but below market rate.

00:42:28.230 --> 00:42:32.158
And so, giving them stock in the company
is a way to compensate them for

00:42:32.158 --> 00:42:35.498
that reduction in cash that
they're potentially seeing.

00:42:35.498 --> 00:42:37.532
So, it increases their upside.

00:42:37.532 --> 00:42:41.136
And it's really important that
you are generous with shares,

00:42:41.136 --> 00:42:43.041
particularly in the early days.

00:42:43.041 --> 00:42:46.637
Because the people who are going to
be employee number one, two, three,

00:42:46.637 --> 00:42:48.551
are going to be with you for a long time,

00:42:48.551 --> 00:42:52.385
they're going to be working really hard
in the same way tat the founders are.

00:42:52.385 --> 00:42:57.075
They're going to be setting
how the company goes forward,

00:42:57.075 --> 00:42:58.585
they're going to be doing a lot of stuff.

00:42:58.585 --> 00:43:02.095
And so, it's important that they
are motivated in the same way.

00:43:03.470 --> 00:43:08.080
And as a rule of thumb, you can think
about giving maybe 10% of the company

00:43:08.080 --> 00:43:12.000
to the first ten employees, but
obviously on a sliding scale, so

00:43:12.000 --> 00:43:15.810
employee one gets more than employee two,
who gets more than employee three, etc.

00:43:17.310 --> 00:43:23.680
And the reason why it's not that much of a
gamble to be generous is that all of these

00:43:23.680 --> 00:43:27.360
will have vesting, all of these employees
will have vesting on their shares.

00:43:27.360 --> 00:43:30.078
So, if something doesn't
actually work out and

00:43:30.078 --> 00:43:34.518
you have to fire the employee, which
when you're hiring people, generally,

00:43:34.518 --> 00:43:38.050
startups make a lot of mistakes and
have to fire a lot of people.

00:43:38.050 --> 00:43:40.209
You're not going to be too burnt by that,

00:43:40.209 --> 00:43:44.663
because the vesting means that the company
has the right to repurchase their shares.

00:43:46.783 --> 00:43:52.610
The way that companies usually structure
this is they create a stock plan,

00:43:52.610 --> 00:43:54.089
which is, again,

00:43:54.089 --> 00:43:59.283
another bunch of legal documents
which need to be signed and kept.

00:43:59.283 --> 00:44:05.805
And they, again, can be done on Clerky or
you can use a lawyer to do those for you.

00:44:05.805 --> 00:44:10.598
And you can either issue shares or you
can issue options from that stock plan.

00:44:10.598 --> 00:44:14.090
And usually what you tend to find is
that employees are issued options,

00:44:14.090 --> 00:44:19.750
which means that they have the option
to pay to buy shares in the future,

00:44:19.750 --> 00:44:21.840
but the price is set now.

00:44:21.840 --> 00:44:23.729
And so, what this means that's good for

00:44:23.729 --> 00:44:26.684
the employees is they don't have
to pay the cash immediately.

00:44:26.684 --> 00:44:31.486
They can actually exercise their options
in the future when the company is looking

00:44:31.486 --> 00:44:35.453
like it's successful or
even in conjunction with an acquisition or

00:44:35.453 --> 00:44:39.161
something, so they don't even
end up any way in out of pocket.

00:44:39.161 --> 00:44:41.760
And there are some tax
consequences to that which,

00:44:41.760 --> 00:44:43.638
it's too much detail to go into now.

00:44:43.638 --> 00:44:48.568
But there's a lot of resources online
about options, about how to issue options,

00:44:48.568 --> 00:44:50.478
about the pros and cons of them.

00:44:50.478 --> 00:44:52.669
So, if you're interested,
there's lots out there.

00:44:55.141 --> 00:44:59.615
Finally, the other thing on this is, it's
really important to communicate to your

00:44:59.615 --> 00:45:02.940
employees what they're
getting in terms of equity.

00:45:02.940 --> 00:45:06.780
So, first of all you need to be clear
to yourself what they're getting.

00:45:06.780 --> 00:45:10.775
Which means that you need to understand
the number of shares they're getting and

00:45:10.775 --> 00:45:13.783
what that represents in terms
of percentage of the company.

00:45:13.783 --> 00:45:16.117
Because if you only give them one and
not the other,

00:45:16.117 --> 00:45:19.715
it gets very confusing because you can
say, you have x percent of the company.

00:45:19.715 --> 00:45:22.160
But what that does mean,
x percent of what?

00:45:22.160 --> 00:45:26.930
Whereas if you say, you have a 100,000
shares, well, what does that mean?

00:45:26.930 --> 00:45:30.341
Is that a lot of the company or
is that a small amount of the company?

00:45:30.341 --> 00:45:35.002
So clear in your head, and then be clear
with the employee about what's going on.

00:45:38.348 --> 00:45:43.604
All right,
final topic is just a couple of reminders

00:45:43.604 --> 00:45:49.587
about things that you have to
do to be a legitimate company.

00:45:49.587 --> 00:45:53.562
So, it's important that you keep all of
these documents that we're talking about

00:45:53.562 --> 00:45:54.367
in a safe place.

00:45:54.367 --> 00:45:56.143
You're going to need
them at stressful times.

00:45:56.143 --> 00:45:58.280
You're going to need
them during acquisitions.

00:45:58.280 --> 00:46:01.086
You're going to need them during
fundraising due diligence.

00:46:01.086 --> 00:46:06.065
You're going to need them so many times,
that if you don't have them all stored in

00:46:06.065 --> 00:46:09.975
signed format, it's just going
to make your life really hard.

00:46:09.975 --> 00:46:14.008
So, keep all your documents in
a safe place just assign one of

00:46:14.008 --> 00:46:16.947
the cofounders to be responsible for this.

00:46:16.947 --> 00:46:20.643
You should make sure you know your key
metrics in the company, which again,

00:46:20.643 --> 00:46:23.900
is going to be covered in a lot more
details through out the course.

00:46:23.900 --> 00:46:25.256
But it's the kind of thing,

00:46:25.256 --> 00:46:28.602
that again, you just want to have on
the top of your head immediately.

00:46:28.602 --> 00:46:34.159
And also, you should make sure that
the money that you're spending,

00:46:34.159 --> 00:46:39.257
that the investors have given to you,
is being spent sensibly.

00:46:39.257 --> 00:46:43.412
Spend money on things that will increase
the chances of the company being

00:46:43.412 --> 00:46:44.100
a success.

00:46:44.100 --> 00:46:47.637
These investors have trusted you
with their money to try to multiply

00:46:47.637 --> 00:46:48.650
their money.

00:46:48.650 --> 00:46:52.270
They haven't given you their
money to go off to Vegas,

00:46:52.270 --> 00:46:56.010
which happened to one of our
companies a few years ago.

00:46:56.010 --> 00:47:00.556
Where one of the founders took quite
a lot of company money, went to Vegas,

00:47:00.556 --> 00:47:03.026
had a great time according to Facebook.

00:47:03.026 --> 00:47:07.770
And the founders and investors found out,
and that founder got fired.

00:47:07.770 --> 00:47:10.100
And actually, he could have
faced criminal charges as well.

00:47:10.100 --> 00:47:13.619
They decided not to press charges,
but just bear in mind that this is

00:47:13.619 --> 00:47:16.785
the company's money and
that's what it should be used for.

00:47:21.418 --> 00:47:27.550
Okay, so in conclusion, that was a really
fast run through of some of the basics.

00:47:28.940 --> 00:47:31.522
Don't expect you to be
experts in any of this.

00:47:31.522 --> 00:47:34.566
Don't expect you to have
all of this figured out.

00:47:34.566 --> 00:47:38.270
But it's just important that you know that
there are some things that you need to do.

00:47:38.270 --> 00:47:43.004
And these items up here are sort
of the really key takeaways for

00:47:43.004 --> 00:47:48.027
this, to just bear in mind as you
go forward with your companies.

00:47:49.820 --> 00:47:53.615
Okay, I think we have a couple of
minutes for questions if there are any.

00:47:58.013 --> 00:48:01.451
&gt;&gt; For the employee.

00:48:01.451 --> 00:48:05.095
Shares, what's generous for,
like the first five employees?

00:48:05.095 --> 00:48:08.536
When you say the word generous?

00:48:08.536 --> 00:48:10.123
&gt;&gt; Well so, you know, it depends,

00:48:10.123 --> 00:48:12.810
because it depends on the kind
of hires that you're giving.

00:48:12.810 --> 00:48:16.035
So if your first hire is going
to be a Software Engineer.

00:48:16.035 --> 00:48:21.555
That's going to be very different if your
first hire is a community manager, say.

00:48:21.555 --> 00:48:26.856
I mean, for the first 10 is usually
somewhere around 10%, maybe for the first

00:48:26.856 --> 00:48:31.960
5, you want to be somewhere in the sort
of 7 to 8 mark, but it really varies.

00:48:31.960 --> 00:48:34.370
And part of this is you
have to think ahead.

00:48:34.370 --> 00:48:37.880
So you don't want to be giving employee
number one 30% of the company,

00:48:37.880 --> 00:48:40.800
and then employee number
two 20% of the company,

00:48:40.800 --> 00:48:44.730
because you're going to run
out of company to give them.

00:48:44.730 --> 00:48:47.920
So it's part of planning ahead for
your hiring needs.

00:48:50.680 --> 00:48:54.910
&gt;&gt; How much transparency should there be
inside the company of the cap table, and

00:48:54.910 --> 00:48:58.200
how should that change as
you grow in employees?

00:48:58.200 --> 00:48:59.060
&gt;&gt; Yeah I mean in the early days,

00:48:59.060 --> 00:49:01.800
where it's just the founders,
there should be 100% transparency.

00:49:01.800 --> 00:49:04.222
Everybody should know everything.

00:49:04.222 --> 00:49:06.865
Generally as the company grows,
it becomes slightly less.

00:49:06.865 --> 00:49:11.225
You wouldn't share a line by line table
to your employees, for insistence.

00:49:11.225 --> 00:49:14.355
But obviously, this transparency is
saying you have this number of shares and

00:49:14.355 --> 00:49:16.852
the total number of and
the total number of shares is this,

00:49:16.852 --> 00:49:19.192
is going to give them
the information that they need.

00:49:19.192 --> 00:49:21.032
Because they only really
care about themselves.

00:49:21.032 --> 00:49:21.982
They don't care.

00:49:21.982 --> 00:49:23.534
Well, actually,
that's not true, is it but.

00:49:23.534 --> 00:49:25.782
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; They should only care about themselves.

00:49:25.782 --> 00:49:29.822
They shouldn't care about what
the person next to them is getting.

00:49:29.822 --> 00:49:34.047
But you wouldn't share that information.

00:49:34.047 --> 00:49:38.757
&gt;&gt; For organizations that may have
a faster path to profitability, and

00:49:38.757 --> 00:49:43.140
a longer path to exit, is it still
best practice to give up equity

00:49:43.140 --> 00:49:46.325
instead of doing things
like profit sharing?

00:49:46.325 --> 00:49:50.819
&gt;&gt; I mean, if you're looking at a startup,
you know, the sort of general start up

00:49:50.819 --> 00:49:54.929
idea of hockey stick growth, and
most of the value is created by equity.

00:49:54.929 --> 00:49:58.780
It's a good idea to generally
compensate people through equity.

00:49:58.780 --> 00:50:01.957
If you're looking at more of something
that's kind of more of a lifestyle

00:50:01.957 --> 00:50:02.479
business.

00:50:02.479 --> 00:50:06.382
Which can be a great business for two or
three founders who don't take outside

00:50:06.382 --> 00:50:09.879
money and create a business that's
very profitable from the start.

00:50:09.879 --> 00:50:12.946
Then yes,
maybe doing some kind of profit share or

00:50:12.946 --> 00:50:17.710
some kind of very generous bonus scheme or
something like is a better answer.

00:50:17.710 --> 00:50:19.600
But usually that's not the case for
startups.

00:50:19.600 --> 00:50:23.557
Usually they're not profitable for
a long time.

00:50:23.557 --> 00:50:28.796
&gt;&gt; How did they insert things different
if you have more of non-for-profit or

00:50:28.796 --> 00:50:33.301
social good based company that's
main prerogative isn't necessarily

00:50:33.301 --> 00:50:35.679
becoming the biggest x in some field?

00:50:35.679 --> 00:50:39.348
&gt;&gt; So nonprofits are very different,
because there isn't this idea of owning

00:50:39.348 --> 00:50:42.950
shares and things, so
that's a whole different idea.

00:50:42.950 --> 00:50:48.140
And there's also rules around how much
you can pay employees in non-profits.

00:50:48.140 --> 00:50:52.266
On the social good companies, it kind of
depends on what sort of a company it is?

00:50:52.266 --> 00:50:57.239
You know again, you need to decide in
a way of, what's your mentality, and

00:50:57.239 --> 00:51:00.364
is your mentality still
to be like a start-up?

00:51:00.364 --> 00:51:04.064
Even though you going to do
some something that gives back,

00:51:04.064 --> 00:51:07.026
you can still have a startup
mentality in that.

00:51:07.026 --> 00:51:12.112
And so you probably still want to go down
that path though of having equity and

00:51:12.112 --> 00:51:14.743
having that conversation in that way.

00:51:14.743 --> 00:51:18.180
And you can still fund raise as well,
as a social good kind of company.

00:51:19.270 --> 00:51:21.850
It's a little bit harder,
but you can still do it.

00:51:24.580 --> 00:51:27.846
&gt;&gt; So let's say you granted those 10% for
the first 10 employees and

00:51:27.846 --> 00:51:30.348
then you are going through
another round of funding.

00:51:30.348 --> 00:51:35.117
Do you dilute only the founders or
also those first employees?

00:51:35.117 --> 00:51:38.893
&gt;&gt; No, so generally,
well this gets a little bit complex, but

00:51:38.893 --> 00:51:43.557
the simple answer is that subsequent
rounds of fundraising will dilute all

00:51:43.557 --> 00:51:47.724
existing shareholders in proportion
to their existing holding.

00:51:47.724 --> 00:51:50.247
It's a little bit more complex than that,
but that's the general.

00:51:52.096 --> 00:51:56.279
&gt;&gt; Yes, so you need to make sure those
employees will know you might be giving

00:51:56.279 --> 00:51:59.325
them shares now that
represents 1% of the company.

00:51:59.325 --> 00:52:03.941
But by the time it gets to an exit,
it might represent 0.01% of the company.

00:52:03.941 --> 00:52:08.512
But in that respect, they probably
have been given more later on, and

00:52:08.512 --> 00:52:11.079
this idea of the pie being much bigger.

00:52:11.079 --> 00:52:16.078
So even, you know, 1% of the company
that's worth zero is still worth much less

00:52:16.078 --> 00:52:19.779
than 0.1% of a company
that's worth $100 million.

00:52:25.120 --> 00:52:30.170
&gt;&gt; Just the salient differences between
preferred shares and the common shares.

00:52:31.500 --> 00:52:34.060
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so
investors get preferred shares and

00:52:34.060 --> 00:52:36.900
as the title suggests they
have some preferences.

00:52:36.900 --> 00:52:40.190
And I'm not a lawyer,
I'm a finance person,

00:52:40.190 --> 00:52:44.390
so I can do this in a very
top level explanation.

00:52:44.390 --> 00:52:47.933
But basically, they have a bunch of
rights that common holders don't have.

00:52:47.933 --> 00:52:51.630
So they have things like liquidation
preferences, which means that,

00:52:51.630 --> 00:52:55.326
when the company exits, they're
first in line to get their proceeds,

00:52:55.326 --> 00:52:59.600
to get the amount of the money that they
invested back, before anybody else does.

00:52:59.600 --> 00:53:04.531
So if, for example, if investors
invested $2 million into a company,

00:53:04.531 --> 00:53:07.961
and then it got sold, but
the cash was, and sorry.

00:53:07.961 --> 00:53:11.901
They invested $2 million into
the company and they earned 20%, but

00:53:11.901 --> 00:53:16.047
the cash that came out of an exit further
down the road was only $2 million.

00:53:16.047 --> 00:53:19.959
They would see all their money before any
of the common holders, ie the founders,

00:53:19.959 --> 00:53:20.900
would see theirs.

00:53:20.900 --> 00:53:22.535
So that's one and

00:53:22.535 --> 00:53:27.350
there's various other different
protections that they have in there.

00:53:27.350 --> 00:53:30.310
That's probably the key role
in the financial basis.

00:53:30.310 --> 00:53:35.387
But then there's voting rights,
there's rights to first refusal.

00:53:35.387 --> 00:53:39.784
There's lots of different things and
that's why it's a much more complex

00:53:39.784 --> 00:53:44.336
negotiation because you have to negotiate
all of these rights within there.

00:53:44.336 --> 00:53:49.317
&gt;&gt; Is there any specific [INAUDIBLE]
where you see it's really successful on

00:53:49.317 --> 00:53:50.877
the [INAUDIBLE] setup?

00:53:50.877 --> 00:53:53.828
And you don't have to say the company,
but who you think?

00:53:53.828 --> 00:53:57.683
There's like a specific motto that you
think works really well for companies and

00:53:57.683 --> 00:53:58.730
schools?

00:53:58.730 --> 00:54:00.070
&gt;&gt; That's a great question.

00:54:01.820 --> 00:54:05.670
I think the companies that do this well
are the ones that are transparent.

00:54:05.670 --> 00:54:11.030
And the ones that sit down with their
employees and really talk through what

00:54:11.030 --> 00:54:14.950
these things mean and
what options are and how vesting works.

00:54:16.090 --> 00:54:19.740
Here's a scenario where
some companies will have,

00:54:20.880 --> 00:54:24.240
if the company exits for this much money,
what does this mean to you?

00:54:24.240 --> 00:54:26.359
Whereas if the company exits for
this much money how will you see?

00:54:26.359 --> 00:54:28.958
And so,
they've done all of these calculations and

00:54:28.958 --> 00:54:31.508
they really make sure that
employees understand.

00:54:31.508 --> 00:54:34.200
And I think that's a really
good practice to have.

00:54:34.200 --> 00:54:37.154
And what that means,
what that needs first,

00:54:37.154 --> 00:54:42.686
is the founders need to understand it,
which is actually surprisingly not common.

00:54:42.686 --> 00:54:46.384
[LAUGH] Often, I have a lot of
conversations with founders where

00:54:46.384 --> 00:54:48.560
they don't understand this stuff.

00:54:49.850 --> 00:54:52.880
They can't even explain it to their
employees, so understanding it yourself

00:54:52.880 --> 00:54:57.050
and then being very clear with the
employees, and being organized as well.

00:54:58.910 --> 00:55:01.610
&gt;&gt; You said you gave say 10% to
the first ten employees, what's-

00:55:01.610 --> 00:55:03.280
&gt;&gt; This is not just a rule of thumb.

00:55:03.280 --> 00:55:04.930
&gt;&gt; Sure [CROSSTALK]
&gt;&gt; Everyone's fixating on it.

00:55:04.930 --> 00:55:07.565
&gt;&gt; My next question,
the next rule of thumb is, after dilution,

00:55:07.565 --> 00:55:10.960
roughly how much of the company
do you think, on average,

00:55:10.960 --> 00:55:14.050
those first employees would end
up owning as an acquisition?

00:55:14.050 --> 00:55:18.830
&gt;&gt; I mean, it really varies because
it depends when in the life cycle of

00:55:18.830 --> 00:55:23.910
the company it gets acquired, it depends
how many fund raising rounds there's been.

00:55:23.910 --> 00:55:28.440
It depends how many more employees
there've been after them.

00:55:28.440 --> 00:55:32.890
So there's a lot of
different variables there.

00:55:32.890 --> 00:55:36.180
I mean, the first money that companies
raise will be the most dilutive.

00:55:36.180 --> 00:55:39.790
So if an employee comes in, and there's
already been three rounds of money.

00:55:39.790 --> 00:55:43.540
Then they're probably not going to be
diluted as much as if they were the first

00:55:43.540 --> 00:55:46.880
employee and then they have to
go through all of that dilution.

00:55:46.880 --> 00:55:48.670
But it's hard to give a number.

00:55:50.550 --> 00:55:54.524
&gt;&gt; So could you throw some light on how
at one shares in Facebook were diluted?

00:55:54.524 --> 00:55:58.864
[INAUDIBLE]
&gt;&gt; No, [LAUGH] I don't know enough of

00:55:58.864 --> 00:56:01.952
the details, [LAUGH] sorry.

00:56:01.952 --> 00:56:06.375
&gt;&gt; Do safe notes usually convert to or
into common equity?

00:56:06.375 --> 00:56:08.180
&gt;&gt; So safes not safe notes.

00:56:10.150 --> 00:56:15.184
They convert usually into preference
shares, because again, the investors

00:56:15.184 --> 00:56:20.158
want all of the rights and privileges
that come with those preferred shares.

00:56:20.158 --> 00:56:22.949
There are some circumstances
where they might convert into

00:56:22.949 --> 00:56:26.031
a combination of preferred and
common, but that's less usual.

00:56:29.752 --> 00:56:33.925
&gt;&gt; Have you seen any different types
of vesting schedules for companies that

00:56:33.925 --> 00:56:38.165
are on longer term timeline where
the cliff might be longer than a year, and

00:56:38.165 --> 00:56:40.740
the total time might be
more than four years?

00:56:41.860 --> 00:56:45.750
&gt;&gt; I sometimes see companies
push it out to five years.

00:56:45.750 --> 00:56:48.780
And also, sometimes companies
don't have the cliff at all and

00:56:48.780 --> 00:56:51.160
they just do monthly
vesting over the period.

00:56:53.730 --> 00:56:56.950
But the longer the time line,
it's a harder sell for

00:56:56.950 --> 00:57:00.830
the employees, and so
you've got to be mindful of that.

00:57:02.020 --> 00:57:05.070
But on the flip side, what usually happens
is, you know if you've had an employee

00:57:05.070 --> 00:57:09.160
who's been with you for four years,
then they've been fairly diluted.

00:57:09.160 --> 00:57:10.830
Because there's going
to be funding rounds,

00:57:10.830 --> 00:57:12.950
there's going to be subsequent employees,
so

00:57:12.950 --> 00:57:16.640
you would actually give them a new stock
grant on top of the one they already have.

00:57:16.640 --> 00:57:19.304
Which would then have new vesting from
the date that you granted it from them.

00:57:19.304 --> 00:57:22.561
So then they'd have another
four year period there, so

00:57:22.561 --> 00:57:27.015
that's kind of how you get along,
how you get through the long time period.

00:57:27.015 --> 00:57:29.280
Okay, last question?

00:57:29.280 --> 00:57:37.112
&gt;&gt; Have you seen any situation where
somebody can trade a common share for

00:57:37.112 --> 00:57:41.901
a preferred share or the other way around?

00:57:41.901 --> 00:57:42.673
Or is that common?

00:57:45.370 --> 00:57:50.564
&gt;&gt; So there wouldn't really be a case
where you would trade common shares for

00:57:50.564 --> 00:57:52.000
preferred shares.

00:57:53.200 --> 00:57:57.600
There is a mechanic in the fundraising,
in the sort of priced round documents,

00:57:57.600 --> 00:58:00.090
this allows preferred shares
to be converted into common

00:58:00.090 --> 00:58:02.600
in various different circumstances.

00:58:02.600 --> 00:58:06.590
But that's usually around exits and
calculation of proceeds and

00:58:06.590 --> 00:58:08.740
things like that.

00:58:08.740 --> 00:58:12.403
Or are you trying to get at
founder's preferred shares?

00:58:12.403 --> 00:58:13.666
&gt;&gt; No, I think you just answered it.

00:58:13.666 --> 00:58:16.586
&gt;&gt; Okay, all right.

00:58:16.586 --> 00:58:17.948
I think we've run out of time.

00:58:17.948 --> 00:58:20.359
So thank you very much for
listening everyone, and

00:58:20.359 --> 00:58:21.771
enjoy the rest of the course.

00:58:21.771 --> 00:58:27.400
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

